<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antony Bek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1245–<a href="/years/1311">1311</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Anthony Bek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antony <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">Bek</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Durham from 1284 and a leading servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>. He took part in the king's Scottish campaigns, including the Battle of Falkirk in 1298, and was made titular patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1306. He exercised the palatine powers of the bishops of Durham with unusual vigour.</p></div>
